this is a production of Cornell  University  thank you Chris good afternoon everybody  it's really my honor to be here and  speak with you today and now that we've  heard about all of these wonderful  veggies and fruits it's upon us with  Francis to bring them to your table and  try to preserve that goodness and that  freshness and I'm going to just tell you  some of the things that we do in food  science to make that happen  now food we do take for granted  oftentimes and we do have as consumers  big expectations when it comes to food  and I try to let here some of the most  important elements that are expected  from food some we think about some we  don't like safety we take it for granted  we buy it from the store we think yes  it's got to be safe from time it's  easier to have it safe than other times  and as food scientists now quite a bit  about that and work in that area so what  we do in food science touches on pretty  much all of these points here safety  nutrition and that's wholesomeness of  the food that gives us enjoyment to  we're trying to work on that too and  then also when we apply these methods of  manufacturing or processing we're trying  to do it in a sustainable  environmentally friendly way and there  are more and more efforts in that area  at Cornell we do work primarily on fruit  and vegetable products and dairy product  but we touch pretty much all food  commodities and I try to capture them on  the slide here I can't forget this  because we are actually speaking of  enjoyment now where we're working in  those areas and they have become quite  popular just a few examples of some of  the work that happens in food science  safety I mentioned earlier that often  times consumers take the you know for  granted and it should be there it should  be in the foods that you buy in the  supermarket or in the local market but  there are things that happen to the food  where it can get contaminated with  bacteria that can make us sick so as a  program we're looking at first of all  how bacteria can get into foods and what  are the bacteria that can make us sick  and how can we prevent them from getting  into our food and we've had programs  like this very successful for for many  years and I just brought it except for  you to see that we are getting  recognition we're getting national and  international recognition for some of  that work our dairy team our dairy  microbiologist and the board has helped  build that program have helped over the  years both consumers and the industry  with achieving safety but we're here to  talk today about what's coming up new  so-called revolution I well I don't know  if we can call it exactly revolution but  certainly we're leading some new ways in  which we can keep our food safe we're  working for instance on some materials  that one can use in manufacturing plants  to prevent bacteria from attaching and  then from from perpetuating this  contamination and you see here a photo  of some bacterial cells on a surface  that in my group we have developed  collaboratively with a group in nano  engineering that doesn't use any  chemicals that just uses the surface  features to prevent bacteria from  sticking to that surface so that in the  end we mitigate contamination and also  use less chemicals to clean the  equipment so it could be in the end a  more sustainable way of doing food  processing something that I'm personally  very excited about is what's happening  now in food science on our both  locations Attica and Geneva we have seen  in recent years an increased interest in  these alternatives new processes there's  not a sort of new processing  technologies where we rely less on heat  to treat the food to make it safe and  and last on the shelf so we do now have  a big initiative here at Cornell where  we are getting some of these new novel  technologies that enable us to keep food  safe without the use of heat or using  minimal heat and we do have for for  example this new high pressure treatment  technologies that we are using to kill  bacteria in certain foods and we have in  Geneva one of the best piece of  equipment that could could be at any  University actually I don't I'm not  aware of many places that have that that  help us help the industry maintain the  safety of foods and more about that in  in a little bit the longest thin line we  are getting some other technologies that  help us make new foods foods that are  fresher and these we hope will help us  restructure the way some foods are being  manufactured we've relied on feet for  you know many decades for centuries now  to make food and that's fine and heat is  actually sometimes you can't you can't  make a food safe without heat but we're  also looking at some of these novel  technologies that will allow us to use  different principles to make food just  one example that I want to show you  because you may not be even be aware  that you're buying already some of the  foods that are made using these  technologies I mentioned high pressure  what do I mean by that well imagine  putting the food on the bottom of the  ocean actually at the deepest point in  the ocean in the Mariana Trench you only  have about a sixth of the total pressure  that we can that we are using now in  food manufacturing to treat foods so  that bacteria is killed and it's been  successful for a number of products and  these are commercial  product that you might have bought and  you didn't realize that they were  treated with high pressures that's how  we can now buy fresh guacamole from from  the store and well it's processed but it  retains that freshness and the variety  of foods you see all of these well  highly expensive right now juices like  six dollar bottle of you know the Suge's  were naked juices all of these very cool  things your green juices that if you had  he treated them they certainly wouldn't  look like that they wouldn't taste as  fresh and they were made possible by  using these technologies  unfortunately correctly you can't put  you know strawberries in that high  pressure unit and still have them  maintain their structure but in in many  cases you can do that now what are we  doing here we're actually helping  understand better which microorganisms  you can kill using this technology which  foods you can make safe and also improve  their shelf life using this technology  and we're trying to also help industry  to adopt this technology in their own  plant but we're also trying to utilize  these new technologies in different ways  because you can now use raw products and  treat them with hydration really use  them to manufacture new types of food  products this is just an image of the  high pressure treatment unit that we  have in Geneva New York but what we are  doing for instance in in my program  right now we're understanding more and  more what the this treatment does to  different cause different chemical  compounds in foods different for  instance proteins so there are some  structural changes that happen and you  can actually take proteins from milk or  take proteins from your your pulses and  and other sources and expose them to  pressure you don't add any ingredients  you don't add any acid you don't add  heat and  yes you can obtain these interesting  structures here that you know you kind  of have a jello type product or yogurt  type product just by views of pressure  and we're leading the way in making  those things happen and these are not  products that you have on the Shelf  right now but we're hoping that will be  part of the making of these products in  the future and they will be both you  know interesting products retain  freshness nutrition and safe at the same  time and with that like to thank you for  your attention this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at cornell edu

